  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1141 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSurface high pressure has begun to slide east today, allowing for\nmore of a southerly component to appear within low-level winds\nthis morning. 925 - 850 mb moisture advection will follow as Gulf\nmoisture is pulled northward. Diurnal mixing will pull this\nmoisture to the surface, leading to dewpoints returning to the\nmiddle to upper 40s, also drastically improving afternoon\nrelative humidity values. Additionally, highs will continue to\nclimb into the 70s. Similar conditions will remain through\nSaturday as upper ridging keeps dry and calm weather in place.\n\nThrough the remainder of the week a large trough will swing\nthrough the Rockies. By Saturday, the trough will become\nelongated into a broad jet streak extending from the southwestern\nCONUS into the northern Plains. This system will steadily march\neastward through the day, but an area of flow on the southwestern\nflank of the jet streak will lag behind, morphing into a low-\namplitude shortwave. This feature will then eject into the\ncentral Plains behind the broader system, traveling east towards \nthe Middle Mississippi River Valley into Sunday. Days of \npersistent southeasterly low-level flow will advect additional \nmoisture into the Mid-South ahead of the shortwave ahead of a cold\nfront. Dewpoints in the upper 50s to low 60s will provide enough \nlow-level theta e to produce MUCAPE in excess of 500 J/kg in \nadvance of the front. Therefore, convection is expected Sunday \nafternoon throughout the region. Effective shear values around 30 \nknots will allow for some updraft organization leading multi- \ncells and transient supercell structures. However, hodographs are \nvery messy with effectively no SRH, leading to any thunderstorm \nimpacts being from isolated damaging wind and hail. The front will\npass through during the evening, exiting the region overnight \nSunday and into Monday morning.\n\nCooler and drier weather will follow behind the cold front as a\npolar high moves into the northern CONUS from Canada, pushing the\nfront into the Gulf. Lows will fall back into the low 30s and\nupper 20s as early as Tuesday morning. A potential tropical\nsystem this weekend off the east coast will also offer to further\nreinforce the cooler air throughout the region through the first\nhalf of next week. Ensembles are beginning to converge on a new\ntrough moving into the region from the northwest, but there are\nstill significant uncertainties in the amplitude of the system,\nleading to additional uncertainties in terms of precipitation\nlate next week. For now, PoPs remain low, but there is growing\npotential for unsettled weather to impact Thanksgiving travel\nacross the southeast and east coast.",
